The display was—and remains in 2024—in the **Geological Court of Crystal Palace Park, south London**, on/alongside one of the islands in the park’s Tidal Lakes, near the pterosaurs. Much of it is difficult to see from the path because it is partly submerged.

The animal was **_Mosasaurus hoffmanni_**, commonly called a mosasaur. Hawkins built only its head, neck, part of the back and one flipper because, in the 1850s, the fossils known from the Netherlands consisted essentially of skull material. The original statue was never completed.

Modern palaeontology shows that the missing body would have been that of a large, fully marine lizard—roughly up to **17 metres long**—with:

- a long, streamlined body;
- four broad, paddle-like flippers;
- a powerful tail ending in a shark-like, vertically oriented tail fin;
- smooth, finely scaled skin; and
- a large predatory head and jaws.

Thus, the Crystal Palace figure is not a complete modern reconstruction: it is a surviving Victorian fragment of a mosasaur, whose submerged “body” was never actually sculpted.
